what is the point in bringing a vote of  no confidence when you know the tories  are not going to vote for it  well we think boris johnson should go  now um and we hope that our the  conservative mps agree with us um on  that you know a lot of them resigned on  mass recently a lot of them have voted  against boris johnson i think the whole  country uh realizes that boris johnson  just has no integrity and honesty i  think it's time for him to go yeah but  they want to they don't want a general  election they want a new prime minister  the vote of no confidence can lead to  the prime minister resigning and if  another conservative mp commands the  confidence of a majority of parliament  then they can take over as an interim  leader until the new leader is appointed  in september but i just don't think  anyone feels comfortable with leaving  boris johnson in position as prime  minister particularly over summer you  know parliament doesn't sit over summer  it's very hard to keep scrutiny on what  he's doing i think don't you worry we'll  keep an eye on him that's good that's  good to hear you know but we'd like to  do that as well  in parliament and we want to make sure  and that he goes as quickly as possible  i think that is the overwhelming feeling  amongst people in the country and i  think the conservatives should take this  opportunity uh to get him out now so why  do you want this vote of no confidence  well we had hoped that the conservative  party in parliament their 1922 committee  would have done the right thing when  they met i think it was last night and  got porridge johnson out of office and  put in place a caretaker leader until  the new person is appointed in september  they didn't do that  and so we now feel forced to table this  vote of no confidence um as the last  opportunity uh to get boris johnson out  of downing street uh before the end of  parliament next week so just to clarify  it's not that you're trying to  push for a general election is because  you're trying to get the prime minister  out of downing street well we want a  general election you know we would love  a general election you know we want uh  that to happen as soon as possible but  this is also an opportunity uh primarily  to get rid of boris johnson and so  conservative mps if they really mean it  that they think boris johnson is not fit  to hold office and they want him out  this is an opportunity for them to get  him out this week they're a bit busy  voting on an election campaign  themselves  well they are busy at that but the  country needs to be run properly um and  i think having someone in 10 downing  street for the next few months who has  totally lost the trust of the people two  months it's until the 5th of september  so it's six weeks or so yeah but it's an  important time there's a huge amount  going on we don't want the government to  be in paralysis over the summer we don't  want the government to be headed up by  someone who's totally lost their  confidence but you guys said that it was  in paralysis for the last few months  well it has been i have it always we  need to get on and deal with the cost of  living crisis we need to make sure that  the government is actually taking a lead  on the issues the country is facing um  and the truth is the prime minister does  not have that confidence of the people  across the country and i think the  conservative mp should do the right  thing and get him out of office uh you  criticize tory leadership candidates  plans for so-called unfunded tax cuts um  in what way are they unfunded and what  are your  wealth tax plans well  there's been a  bonanza of fantasy economics over the  last few days you know conservative  leadership hopefuls have been falling  over themselves to promise tax cuts and  if you top them all up you know there's  some kind of 330 billion pounds worth of  unfunded spending commitments  which you see not chasm well richie  sunak has his own credibility problems  you know he's been talking about wanting  to cut taxes but people only have to  look at his immediate record as  chancellor of the last few months which  is that he raised  taxes on working people through  increasing national insurance right in  the middle of a cost of living crisis  making us the only g7 economy to raise  taxes on working people in this cost of  living crisis um the windfall tax which  we've been saying should be going toward  helping with the cost of living he was  delaying supporting that for months and  months he's had the wrong calls and all  the big issues that we've been facing  and so i don't think he has any  credibility either um i spoke to nadim  zahawi at length also my colleague beth  robie has spoken to satya javid and both  of them are saying that they would cut  the fat from government departments and  that would be enough to fund these tax  cuts that they're talking about  well i mean it's hard to keep track of  all of their unfunded spending  commitments there are 330 billion pounds  worth of them you know i saw some clips  of what nadine sahar we told you on your  show um yesterday and he seemed to be  talking about you know 20 cuts across  the board you know but he has to lay out  real plans for this you know does he  mean 20 cuts to the nhs you know how  many doctors and nurses is mean that  does he mean 20 does he mean 20 cuts to  defense does he mean 20 percent cut to  schools what does he mean by it  is within the operating daily operating  of the department who's watching this  this is just fantasy economics you know  these are not credible he said he did it  at the department of education and it  worked he needs to lay out his plans if  he wants to be prime minister of how  he's going to implement what he's saying  he's going to do and the truth is this  is all make-believe this is all fantasy  this is all  a desperate attempt by no deems of  harvey and all the other conservative  candidates to distance themselves from  boris 